#
# prima-materia — graded attainment and the same two capacities, across traditions
#
# The correspondence is narrow on purpose. Two traditions that catalogue
# capacities agree on two of them and on the form of the claim, and they are
# not the same two traditions that any diffusion account would put together.
#

pmc:GradedAttainmentYieldsDistantPerceptionAndOtherMinds a pm:Converging ;
    rdfs:label "A graded attainment is said to yield hearing at a distance and knowledge of other minds"@en, "Eine gestufte Erlangung soll Fernhören und Kenntnis fremden Bewusstseins hervorbringen"@de ;
    skos:definition "Two transmissions state the same claim in the same form. A named procedure, entered by stages, is said to produce two named capacities: perception of what is beyond the range of the senses, and knowledge of what is in another mind. In the one the procedure is the sustained application of attention to a single object, and the capacities follow verse by verse. In the other the procedure is an initiation into a graded society, and the capacities are read off the marks of the degree."@en ;
    pm:convergingConcept pmc:MideDistantHearing, pmc:DivyaSrotra, pmc:MideThoughtReading, pmc:ParacittaJnana ;
    pm:independentAttestation pmt:Midewiwin ;
    skos:note "What makes this worth holding is not the capacities, which are common, but the form of the claim. Both traditions bind the capacity to a procedure and to a place in a sequence, rather than ascribing it to a figure or a birth. That form is what a protocol can address; an ascription to a hero is not."@en ;
    skos:note "The contact question, carefully. No route is proposed by anyone between the Anishinaabe of the upper Great Lakes and the Sanskrit commentarial literature, and the dispute recorded at pmc:MidewiwinAgeDispute does not touch this: whether the society formed before European contact or in reaction to it, a seventeenth-century Great Lakes formation has no access to the third pāda either way. What the dispute does affect is the age of the Midewiwin claim, not its independence."@en ;
    skos:note "The one real objection to independence lies in the recording and not in the traditions, and it is stated here rather than left for a reader to find. The Midewiwin account was written down between eighteen eighty-seven and eighteen eighty-nine, by which time the capacities of the third pāda had been in English-language circulation for a decade through theosophical publishing. Two things weigh against the objection. The recorder was a bureau ethnographer and not a member of that milieu, and the capacities are given as readings of lines incised on birchbark charts — marks that were on the bark before anyone asked what they meant. The objection is not thereby closed; it is entered so that it can be closed or sustained by someone who reads the charts."@en ;
    skos:note "Two differences that a correspondence must not smooth. The Indian text ranks its capacities as obstacles with respect to the goal of the practice; the American report names no such rank and warns instead against the member who turns them to harm. And the Indian claim is a claim about attention, repeatable in principle by anyone who applies it, where the American one is a claim about an institution, obtainable only from priests and only for a fee that the report says impoverishes the candidate. The capacity is the same; what it costs and what it is for are not."@en ;
    dcterms:source "Walter James Hoffman, The Midē'wiwin or Grand Medicine Society of the Ojibwa, Seventh Annual Report of the Bureau of Ethnology (Washington: Government Printing Office, 1891), pp. 168 and 170", "Patañjali, Yogasūtra III.19", "Patañjali, Yogasūtra III.41" .
